WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.317
Choosing a join becomes easier when you translate the question into a preservation rule.

00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:13.442
If the question is only about patients who have admissions, then requiring a match is appropriate,

00:00:13.442 --> 00:00:18.527
so INNER JOIN fits. If the question says every patient, whether or not an admission exists,

00:00:18.527 --> 00:00:21.452
then the patient population must be preserved.

00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:24.632
Put patients on the left and use LEFT JOIN.

00:00:24.632 --> 00:00:30.427
If the question says every department, including departments with no staff or admissions,

00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:36.467
preserve departments. If you need to compare two sources and see unmatched records from both sides,

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:39.332
FULL OUTER JOIN is a natural choice.

00:00:39.332 --> 00:00:47.552
Pay attention to words such as every, only matched, including those with none, and unmatched from both sides.

00:00:47.552 --> 00:00:54.212
Those phrases are clues. For example, a manager might ask, show me every product and its total sales,

00:00:54.212 --> 00:00:55.697
including products with zero sales.

00:00:55.697 --> 00:01:01.142
That is not the same question as show me products that sold this month.

00:01:01.142 --> 00:01:07.162
The first one needs preservation of the product table.

00:01:07.162 --> 00:01:09.652
The second can require a match.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:14.832
SQL syntax becomes much easier when you learn to hear the preservation requirement inside the business

00:01:14.832 --> 00:01:16.240
language.
